// Reagents — the four working solutions and the standard. Content markup only.

= Reagents

All solutions are made up in glass-distilled water and keep indefinitely unless
noted.

== Reagent A

Two per cent sodium carbonate in 0.10 N sodium hydroxide.

== Reagent B

Half a per cent cupric sulphate pentahydrate ($"CuSO"_4$) in one per cent sodium
or potassium tartrate. The tartrate holds the copper in solution in the alkaline
medium; without it a precipitate forms on mixing.

== Reagent C — alkaline copper

Prepared fresh each day by adding one volume of reagent B to fifty volumes of
reagent A. The mixture is stable for about a day, after which the copper slowly
comes out of the tartrate complex.

== Folin–Ciocalteu phenol reagent

The commercial reagent, diluted with water to make it one normal in acid. A
titration against standard alkali to a phenolphthalein end-point fixes the
dilution; the working strength is about 1 N.

== Standard protein

Crystalline bovine serum albumin, dried to constant weight, dissolved to give a
stock of $200 #h(0.1em) mu"g"$ per millilitre. A fresh standard curve is run with
each batch of unknowns, since the absolute colour yield drifts a little with the
age of the phenol reagent.
